<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zhanguo ce</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a> · Also known as Strategies of the Warring States, Zhan Guo Ce</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Zhanguo ce, or Strategies of the Warring States, is a Chinese collection of speeches, anecdotes and diplomatic schemes from the fifth to the third century BCE, arranged by state. It was compiled by the Han scholar Liu Xiang in the first century BCE from earlier materials. Although often rhetorical rather than strictly factual, it is a major source for the politics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/zhanguo-ce.jpg" alt="Zhanguo ce" width="320" height="550" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Su Qin, Liu Hsziang · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ZhanGuoCe.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>While editing the imperial library, Liu Xiang found six versions of writings from the School of Diplomacy, in poor condition, with confused contents and missing words, and edited them into one book under the title Zhan Guo Ce. It is therefore generally held, after Zhang Xincheng, that the book was not written by one author at one time; it is thought to have been composed by Su Qin and his peers. In 1931 Luo Genze argued that Kuai Tong, a contemporary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-xin">Han Xin</a>, was its author, a view refuted by Zhang Xincheng in 1939.</p><p>Much of the text was lost in later centuries, and Zeng Gong of the Northern Song dynasty recovered some lost chapters and edited the version read today. Unlike many pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> classics, its authenticity was not questioned from the Western Han period onward; the compiler of the Annotated Bibliography of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siku-quanshu">Complete Library of the Four Treasuries</a> was perhaps the first to call it apocryphal, without giving grounds.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The book runs from the conquest of the Fan clan by the Zhi clan in 490 BCE to the failed attempt on the life of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-shi-huang">Qin Shi Huang</a> by Gao Jianli in 221 BCE. Its anecdotes illustrate the strategies and tricks of the Warring States rather than tell the whole history of the period. There is no strict year-by-year dating as in the Spring and Autumn Annals: stories are ordered by the ruler under whom they fall, but within one reign there is no telling whether a day or a year separates two of them. It runs to about 120,000 words in 33 chapters and 497 sections, arranged by twelve states.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>In 1973 writings on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> were excavated from the Han tomb at Mawangdui near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> and published in 1976 as the Zhanguo Zonghengjia Shu. Of their 27 chapters, 11 resemble passages of the Zhan Guo Ce and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/records-of-the-grand-historian">Records of the Grand Historian</a>; the copy was probably made around 195 BCE, since it avoids the word bang, the personal name of Emperor Gao of Han.</p><p>Since the 12th century writers have debated whether the book counts as history, until scholars such as Zhong Fengnian showed in 1936 that it was written as a handbook of rhetoric for the School of Diplomacy. Its stress on power politics has been criticised as at odds with Confucian ideology, while its wit and vigorous style have earned it a place as literature. English translations include James I. Crump's of 1970.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zhan Guo Ce” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Warring States period its armies were very large; the <b>Zhanguo ce</b> credits it with a million soldiers, a rhetorical figure.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-chu">State of Chu · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its capital, Linzi, became perhaps the largest city of the Warring States period: a speech recorded in the <b>Zhanguo ce</b> credits it with 70,000 households and describes streets so crowded that chariot hubs knocked together, and inhabitants who played music, raced dogs and played the board game liubo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-qi">State of Qi · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
